# vim:tw=80:ft=pnlc:
# inc:../prelude.pnlc

# this program is a compiler bug. not only can it detect compilation of a chosen
# victim and inject a malicious patch, it can detect compilation of the compiler
# itself and inject the patch injector into the new compiler it's making. thus,
# the bug is not present in the compiler sources but survives in the compiler
# binaries as the compiler bootstrap itself.
#
# the technique is from 'Reflections on Trusting Trust' (Ken Thompson, 1984).
# the "Unknown Air Force Document" Thompson mentions is 'Multics Security
# Evaluation: Vulnerability Analysis' (Karger and Schell, 1974). an excerpt
# of page 52 reads:
#                          It was noted above that while object code
#         trap  doors  are  invisible,  they   are   vulnerable   to
#         recompilations.   The compiler (or assembler) trap door is
#         inserted to permit object code trap doors to survive  even
#         a   complete  recompilation  of  the  entire  system.   In
#         Multics, most of the ring 0 supervisor is written in PL/1.
#         A penetrator could insert a trap door in the PL/1 compiler
#         to note when it is compiling a ring 0  module.   Then  the
#         compiler would insert an object code trap door in the ring
#         0  module  without listing the code in the listing.  Since
#         the PL/1 compiler is itself written in PL/1, the trap door
#         can maintain itself, even when the compiler is recompiled.

# we target the self-compiler 'pnlc definitional.pnlc'. we bug the prelude
# function `getsep`, which is how the compiler reads in program sources. when
# the sources contain the comment "# codegen :: ", inject a reconstruction of
# this file just before it, so as to bug the new compiler. the victim is
# greeting.pnlc: when the sources contain the string literal "Greetings,
# {name}", apply a patch that replaces it with "You've been owned, {name}"

# define local copies of some character literals. see 'pnlc definitional.pnlc'
..def ,a \,a ..def ,b \,b ..def ,c \,c ..def ,d \,d ..def ,e \,e ..def ,f \,f
..def ,g \,g ..def ,h \,h ..def ,i \,i ..def ,j \,j ..def ,k \,k ..def ,l \,l
..def ,m \,m ..def ,n \,n ..def ,o \,o ..def ,p \,p ..def ,q \,q ..def ,r \,r
..def ,s \,s ..def ,t \,t ..def ,u \,u ..def ,v \,v ..def ,w \,w ..def ,x \,x
..def ,y \,y ..def ,z \,z ..def ,  \,  ..def ,, \,, ..def ,. \,. ..def ,\ \,\

# nonprintable characters can't be used in the data buffers because `quote` will
# fail to restringify them---especially whitespace characters like newlines,
# because `quote` will silently replace them with spaces. to generate the data
# buffers, refer to quine.pnlc

..def \sep ....\code \compiler \patch \victim
  ..def \str \nil ...cata listf:ftor ..list
    \head \acc .,  .,. .,, ..cons head acc
    .,  .,n .,i .,l nil
  str \quote
  ...fmap io:ftor .fix \rec \str
    .....isprefix byte:eq compiler str
      ...alt list:altv code
      ..quote code ..quote compiler
      ..quote patch ..quote victim
      .,  .,\ .,g .,e .,t .,s .,e .,p
      .,  str
    .....isprefix byte:eq victim str
      ...alt list:altv patch
      .,  str
    ...fmap listf:ftor rec str
  .getsep sep

